.TH ELECTRICITY 1 "2026-11-13" "electricity 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
electricity \- run a Circuitry orchestration directly (preview)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
electricity <config.json> <orchestration.yml> [-e key=value]... [--out state.json]
                    [--pretty] [--live-state state.json] [--events events.jsonl]
                    [--profile <path>]
       electricity <config.json> <orchestration.yml> --dump-ir [-e key=value]...
       electricity run <orchestration.yml> [-e key=value]... [--config config.json]
                    [--out state.json] [--live-state state.json] [--events events.jsonl]
                    [--allow-capabilities cap,cap] [--adapter name] [--label TEXT] [--quiet]
                    [--pretty] [--profile <path>] [--state state.json] [--resume MODE] [--force]
       electricity man [run] [--roff]
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
electricity is a preview: it runs
tool/dynamic/if/finally/loop/use/yield/prompt/reflector documents only
(persistence and runtime plugins are refused with a preview marker; use `cof
run` for those). A prompt effect's model is reached for real through a
document's own `adapter:` or a prompt's own `provider:`, including `ollama`,
the OpenAI-compatible family, `openai`, `anthropic`, `replicate`, `watsonx`,
`cyberdiner`, `claude_code`, `pi`, and the `scripted` adapter; `litellm` has
no electricity builder and is refused permanently.
.PP
`electricity run <doc>` accepts the same options `cof run` does, with the same
config discovery (`--config` optional: CIRCUITRY_CONFIG, then a trusted
project config in the working directory, then the global config) and the same
meaning for --allow-capabilities/--adapter/--quiet. The legacy `electricity
<config.json> <doc>` form (config always explicit, never discovered) keeps
working as an alias; --profile/--pretty/--dump-ir are electricity-only
additions, available under either form.
.PP
`electricity man [run]` shows the electricity(1) or electricity-run(1) man
page through `man` on a terminal, or prints its roff when piped or given
--roff.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B -V, --version
Print the version and exit. Legacy form only.
.TP
.B -h
Print this message and exit. Legacy form only; use the long --help under run.
.TP
.B --help
Print this message and exit.
.TP
.B -e
\fIkey=value\fR Pass an orchestration input, checked against its declared
interface.inputs. Repeatable; a later -e for the same key wins.
.TP
.B --out, -o
\fIpath\fR Write the final state to <path> instead of stdout. The -o alias is
run only.
.TP
.B --pretty
Sort state keys and indent by 2 spaces in the final state, whether printed to
stdout or written to --out.
.TP
.B --live-state
\fIpath\fR Mirror the running state to <path> as it changes.
.TP
.B --events
\fIpath\fR Write a JSONL event stream to <path>.
.TP
.B --allow-capabilities
\fIcap,cap\fR Comma-separated capabilities to pre-approve for this run.
.TP
.B --adapter
\fIname\fR This run's default adapter, beating a loaded --profile, the
orchestration and the config.
.TP
.B --quiet
Suppress non-essential output (accepted for parity).
.TP
.B --profile
\fIpath\fR Load a profile (adapter/model/inputs/persistence overrides, routing
pins and provider denials) from an explicit path -- no name discovery.
.TP
.B --dump-ir
Print the compiled IR as JSON and exit. Unstable: a debugging aid that can
change in any release. Legacy form only.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B electricity config.json orchestration.yml -e name=world --pretty
Runs a document directly, with one input, and a sorted/indented final state on
stdout.
.TP
.B electricity run orchestration.yml --out state.json
Runs a document through the same config discovery and options cof run uses,
writing the final state to a file instead of stdout.
.TP
.B electricity run orchestration.yml --state state.json --resume continue
Resumes a previous run from its saved state, skipping any named effect that
already finished without error.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B CIRCUITRY_CONFIG
A config JSON path, for electricity run's own discovery (--config takes
priority over it; a trusted project config and then the global config are
tried after it). The legacy form's first positional is always explicit and
never consults this variable.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/circuitry/config.json
The global config, the last layer electricity run's own discovery tries.
.TP
.I ~/.config/circuitry/.env
Private environment variables, loaded before config resolution (the same file
cof setup writes).
.TP
.I ~/.config/circuitry/trusted.json
Project configs trusted by cof trust; electricity run's own discovery consults
it the same way cof run does.
.TP
.I ./circuitry.config.json, ./config.json
A project config in the working directory, used only once trusted.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
The run failed, the orchestration was not found, or the config could not be
read.
.TP
.B 2
A usage error -- an unrecognized option, a missing required argument, or an
unknown electricity man page.
.TP
.B 129
The run was interrupted by SIGHUP.
.TP
.B 130
The run was interrupted by SIGINT (Ctrl-C).
.TP
.B 143
The run was interrupted by SIGTERM.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR electricity (1),
.BR electricity\-run (1),
.BR cof (1),
.BR cof\-run (1),
.BR osi (1)
